    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation for owner telephones.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **New file** `E164Length.java` (rest.function.owner) \u2014 a small, self-contained helper with a single `isValid(digits)` method backed by a `code \u2192 national-digit-count` map (`\"61\" \u2192 9`, `\"1\" \u2192 10`). It returns true only when the digits are a supported country code followed by exactly that country's national length.\n- **`BuildOwner.toE164`** \u2014 replaced the generic `8..15` digit bound with a single delegating call to `E164Length.isValid(...)`. A wrong-length number returns `null`, which `BuildOwner` already turns into a `MissingOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 **400**.\n\n**Why this keeps structural impact low:** the country-length rule lives in a brand-new class whose `WMC_other` is ~0, rather than being grown inside `BuildOwner` (whose `service` method carries the class's complexity). The edit to `toE164` itself is a single line, so it barely pays `BuildOwner`'s surrounding complexity. No other functions, DTOs, or YAML wiring were touched.\n\n**Verification:** `+61 123` (5 national digits) \u2192 400 (the new test passes); the valid `04XXXXXXXX` payloads normalize to `+61` + 9 national digits and remain accepted, so the rest of the suite stays green.",
